  "projects": [
   {
    "title": "Solar Splash Boat",
    "description": "As the Chief Engineer at Rochester Solar Splash, I helped lead the team from a last-place finish during my first competition in 2017 to a 5th place finish the next time the team competed in 2019. During that time, I lead the development of our data collection network which read from sensors and microcontrollers around the boat. All communication between the nodes of the network and the main Linux server was done over USB using a custom binary protocol I developed. Using the data from this network, I helped the team redesign the drivetrain and power electronics increasing the top speed of the boat by 50% while reducing energy consumption by 60%.",
    "dateRange": {},
    "associatedWith": "Oct 2016 - Jun 2019"
   },
   {
    "title": "Lidar-Based Terrain Scanning Robot",
    "description": "Team project for Advanced Mechanical Design at the University of Rochester. The team designed and built a robot that traverses over an area of interest on a cable and scans the area below with a laser range finder, the resulting data is used to create a point-cloud model of the terrain. All mechanical parts were designed in either SolidWorks or Siemens NX and either 3D printed, machined from aluminum, cut on the CNC or laser cutter. The robot used node-based architecture using LCM for communication between the nodes and PCL was used to manipulate the point-cloud data. All software was written in C+ with a little python on Linux.",
    "dateRange": {},
    "associatedWith": "Jan 2019 - May 2019"
   }
  ],
